Author: coordinator <coordinator@local>
Date:   Wed Sep 30 13:41:26 2026 -0400

    fix(swift): lambda return contract, with the block destination it exposes
    
    Step (b) of the integration ruling, as its own atomic commit. The two halves
    cannot be separated: the lambda reseed is what makes blockExpression's read of
    currentReturnType wrong, so the repair that keeps the reseed correct ships with
    it or not at all. That is why this is one commit and not two.
    
    WHAT IT DOES
     - functionLiteral seeds currentReturnType from f.t around functionLiteralInner
       and restores it, so the return-position sites take the lambda's own contract
       instead of the enclosing member's (d14aae11's intent, previously withdrawn
       as a14345ce).
     - blockExpression no longer reads currentReturnType. A block's value is its OWN
       declared result type, not any enclosing return contract.
    
    THE CONSTRUCTIVE FINDING that makes the second half correct rather than
    convenient: blockExpression is reached ONLY from argument position. At return and
    initializer position Haxe splices an inline helper's statements into the
    enclosing block, so TBlock never appears. The read-only boundary a consumer needs
    is applied by the consumer around the whole IIFE - measured as
    viewLen(ReadOnlyArray(<IIFE>)) - so the removed conversion was not merely the
    wrong contract, it was redundant. The narrow alternative of excluding
    blockExpression from the reseed was rejected deliberately: it would have
    preserved a pre-existing member-scope defect and made the same value render
    differently depending on whether it sits inside a lambda.
    
    MEASURED, with swiftc -c rather than -typecheck (a recorded trap: -typecheck
    skips SILGen and cannot surface this class):
    
      probe            pre-fix   HEAD    fixed
      MXProbe (P4)      rc=0     rc=1     rc=0
      MXBlockMember     rc=1     rc=1     rc=0   (member-scope control; already red)
      MXLambda witness  rc=1     rc=0     rc=0, runs A=2 D.count=2 first=a G=2/1 J=2/1 K=2
    
    ALSO IN THIS COMMIT: tests/swift-gap-boundary/ enters the repository. It had
    existed only in scratch worktrees, so the counterexample fixture was never
    collected by any command - which is precisely the collection gap contract 3
    describes, and it is why ARCHITECTURAL-CONTRACTS.md:65 cited a directory that
    did not exist. Its warning pin has been discharged and removed, exactly as the
    gate-owner ruling recorded in that file said it would be: W1 is on the line, the
    two [#no-usage] warnings are gone, and three independent sessions measured 0/0.
    What replaces the pin is the assertion it existed to defer, plus an explicit
    record of what is still unmet - see below. Run: `bun test tests/swift-gap-boundary/`
    -> 2 pass / 0 fail, rc=0, printing "recorded 0 pending build-phase diagnostic(s)".
    
    RECORDED, NOT WAIVED: per the round-145 gate-owner ruling, a build-phase
    diagnostic counts against 02-translator-implementation-standard.md:78/:80, and
    under `swiftc -c` this fixture emits one `will never be executed` at Gap.swift:117
    (W1's unreachable trailing return). The fixture therefore asserts zero type
    errors and the absence of the W1 warnings, records any pending build-phase
    diagnostics to a log rather than hiding them, and keeps ZERO DIAGNOSTICS UNDER -c
    as the stated goal. This is an unwaived deviation, not compliance.
    
    Not measured for this change: adjacent holes the same report named (a
    single-return lambda fast path that never applies a boundary conversion, and the
    try-return gate, unchanged), and the full bun suite, whose outcome is inherited
    from BASELINE-FAILURES.md.

